WEBVTT

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:03.530
Setting up a lab?

00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:07.888
The most common question we hear is: HPL or stainless steel?

00:00:08.168 --> 00:00:10.299
Here is how to decide in one minute.

00:00:10.899 --> 00:00:13.326
Choose stainless everywhere and you overspend.

00:00:13.606 --> 00:00:18.273
Choose laminate everywhere and a hot press or harsh chemical can damage it.

00:00:18.873 --> 00:00:22.125
High-pressure laminate suits dry and semi-wet labs,

00:00:22.125 --> 00:00:26.205
where chemicals are handled often but are not highly aggressive.

00:00:26.485 --> 00:00:31.225
It resists solvents and mild acids, is easy to clean, and costs less.

00:00:31.505 --> 00:00:37.496
Stainless steel is the choice for heat, corrosive exposure, hygiene, and machines that run hot.

00:00:37.776 --> 00:00:42.754
Many labs need both, on different benches, depending on what each instrument does.

00:00:43.354 --> 00:00:49.341
In one oil and gas materials lab we equipped, the analytical instruments sit on HPL benches,

00:00:49.341 --> 00:00:53.832
while the hot mount press and grinder sit on a stainless steel bench.

00:00:54.432 --> 00:00:57.539
So you can mix surfaces in one lab, bench by bench.

00:00:57.819 --> 00:00:59.440
Which instruments are in your lab?

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:04.736
Comment below, and next, learn the difference between a rubber mat and an ESD mat.

00:01:05.336 --> 00:01:09.581
Furnisys: complete workspace systems, planned around your process.

